# mgsettlr.msg - the generic Megaton settler (mgsettlr.ssl).
# 100-103 look; floating lines: 110-127 bomb dormant, 130-143 disarmed, 150-163 rigged,
# 170-184 Simms dead, 186-189 small things, 190-197 night, 200-213 answers to the sermon;
# 300+ dialogue. One entry per line; no braces in comments.

{100}{}{You see a man of Megaton, sunburned and in no hurry.}
{101}{}{You see a woman of Megaton, sunburned and in no hurry.}
{102}{}{Patched clothes, a tin cup on the belt, and the squint of someone who lives beside a bomb and stopped noticing years ago.}
{103}{}{Standing at the edge of the glowing pool, eyes half shut, lips moving along with the sermon.}

# 110-125: the bomb is dormant. 113 and 120 have the stand-ins 126 and 127.
{110}{}{Bomb's still there. I checked. I check every day.}
{111}{}{Don't drink from the puddle. I know it's free.}
{112}{}{Moriarty's watering the beer again. With what, I don't ask.}
{113}{}{Walter says the pipes will hold. Walter's been saying that since I was small.}
{114}{}{The robot greeted my shadow twice today.}
{115}{}{Wind's shifting. Somebody ask the preacher to shout the other way.}
{116}{}{Rent's cheap. You get used to the ticking.}
{117}{}{Sheriff walks the same line every day. You could set a clock by the hat.}
{118}{}{If it goes off, at least it's quick. That's the town motto. Unofficially.}
{119}{}{Stahl's stew has meat in it today. Nobody's asking what kind.}
{120}{}{That suit in the saloon tips like a man who's never been hungry.}
{121}{}{Caravan's due. Hide your good boots or they'll make you an offer.}
{122}{}{Moira asked me to hold a mole rat for her. Just hold it. I said no.}
{123}{}{Doc says I've got a touch of the glow. He says it like it's my fault.}
{124}{}{My brother went to the Den to make his fortune. The Den kept him.}
{125}{}{Wall's holding. Wall always holds. Knock on tin.}
{126}{}{Water's coming clear at last. I'd forgotten it had no color.}
{127}{}{Saloon's the same as ever. Warm beer, cold looks.}

# 130-143: the bomb is disarmed.
{130}{}{Slept like a baby. First time since I moved in.}
{131}{}{They say a stranger pulled the bomb's teeth. Walked up and did it.}
{132}{}{It's only a lump of iron now. The kids will be climbing it by spring.}
{133}{}{The preacher's taking it hard. Shouts louder, means it less.}
{134}{}{Funny. I miss the ticking.}
{135}{}{Rent's going up. You watch. Safe costs extra.}
{136}{}{Sheriff smiled yesterday. I thought he'd hurt himself.}
{137}{}{Moriarty's selling a drink called the Dud. Same beer, double the price.}
{138}{}{Robot's taking credit for the dead bomb. I never saw him holding a wrench.}
{139}{}{Caravans will stop longer, now there's nothing to hurry away from.}
{140}{}{I used to leave the door open, for running. Now I just leave it open.}
{141}{}{Who'd have thought. All it wanted was somebody who could read a wire.}
{142}{}{Dead bomb, live puddle. Don't get comfortable.}
{143}{}{Somebody ought to plant something on it. Flowers. A flag. Moriarty.}

# 150-163: Burke's charge is fitted. Nobody knows; everybody feels it.
{150}{}{Did the bomb always hum like that?}
{151}{}{My fillings ache. I haven't got fillings.}
{152}{}{The rats are leaving the plant shed. All of them. In a line.}
{153}{}{Preacher says Atom's stirring. He says a lot. He never looked scared before.}
{154}{}{Something's different. Can't put a finger on it. Don't want to.}
{155}{}{The puddle's brighter. Tell me the puddle isn't brighter.}
{156}{}{There's a man in a suit out by the caravan fire. Just standing. Watching the wall.}
{157}{}{I've half a mind to visit my sister in Klamath. Today, maybe.}
{158}{}{The ticking's changed. It used to wander. Now it's counting.}
{159}{}{Couldn't sleep. Whole town felt like it was holding its breath.}
{160}{}{Somebody was at the bomb last night. I heard the plate clang.}
{161}{}{The milk turned. I don't even keep milk.}
{162}{}{Sheriff ought to take a look at that bomb. Somebody tell the sheriff.}
{163}{}{Everything's fine. Robot says so. Everything's fine.}

# 170-184: Simms is dead. 177 has the stand-in 184.
{170}{}{Shot in the back, in the saloon. In our saloon.}
{171}{}{The boy's sitting on the step with his father's hat. Nobody knows what to say.}
{172}{}{Who keeps the peace now? Stockholm can't shoot everybody.}
{173}{}{He was the one man in town Moriarty couldn't buy.}
{174}{}{We ought to have backed him up. We ought to have done a lot of things.}
{175}{}{A town without a sheriff is just a crowd with a wall.}
{176}{}{They laid his star on the bar. Moriarty moved it to pour a drink.}
{177}{}{The suit walked right out the gate. The robot wished him a pleasant journey.}
{178}{}{Every town I ever lived in died of something. I'd hoped this one would be the bomb.}
{179}{}{Doc couldn't do a thing. Says he was gone before he fell.}
{180}{}{I keep expecting to hear his boots.}
{181}{}{Lock your door tonight. Never thought I'd say that here.}
{182}{}{He gave everybody one warning. Nobody gave him one.}
{183}{}{Preacher said a prayer for him. First thing those two ever agreed on.}
{184}{}{Lucas Simms. Never thought I'd see that hat with nobody under it.}

# 186-189: small things.
{186}{}{Somebody gave Micky a drink of water. He cried. Embarrassing for everybody.}
{187}{}{Hello, neighbor. If your roof leaks, that's tradition.}
{188}{}{That suit left town angry. Angry men with money come back with friends.}
{189}{}{Water's stopped tasting of pipe. I almost miss it.}

# 190-197: night. 190 has the stand-in 197.
{190}{}{Can't sleep. The bomb ticks louder at night.}
{191}{}{Too hot in the bunkhouse, too cold out here. That's Megaton.}
{192}{}{You can see the pool glow on a dark night. Pretty, if you don't think.}
{193}{}{Moriarty's still open. Moriarty's always open.}
{194}{}{Quiet. I don't trust quiet.}
{195}{}{Stockholm's awake up there. Probably.}
{196}{}{Go to bed, stranger. Nothing good happens here after dark. Nothing happens at all.}
{197}{}{Stars are out. Wall's up. Good enough.}

# Answers to the sermon: 200-205 dormant, 206-209 disarmed, 210-213 rigged.
{200}{}{Atom be praised.}
{201}{}{In the Glow we stand.}
{202}{}{Blessed be the Division.}
{203}{}{Tell it, Confessor.}
{204}{}{He sleeps. He sleeps.}
{205}{}{My feet have gone numb. Praise Atom.}
{206}{}{He only sleeps.}
{207}{}{Sleep well, Atom.}
{208}{}{We wait. We wait.}
{209}{}{Softly now.}
{210}{}{He stirs!}
{211}{}{...Is it supposed to do that?}
{212}{}{I felt it too, Confessor.}
{213}{}{Should we be standing further back?}

# Greetings.
{300}{}{New face. Don't touch the bomb, don't drink the puddle, don't play cards with Moriarty. That's the tour.}
{301}{}{Still here? The town grows on you. Like a rash, but it grows.}
{302}{}{You're the one who killed the bomb. I had money on you blowing us all up. Glad to lose it.}
{303}{}{We've just lost our sheriff. Folks aren't feeling chatty. I'm folks.}
{304}{}{You feel that? Like the air before a dust storm. No? Just me, then.}
{305}{}{It's late. Whatever you're selling, sell it tomorrow.}
{306}{}{Hello, neighbor. Heard you got the old place by the gate. Roof's sound. Mostly.}
{307}{}{What else?}
{308}{}{Let's talk about something else.}
{309}{}{Where else can a stranger go?}

# Hub options.
{310}{}{What is there to do in Megaton?}
{311}{}{Heard anything interesting?}
{312}{}{Why would anyone live next to a bomb?}
{313}{}{Hullo.}
{314}{}{Be seeing you.}
{315}{}{Bye-bye.}
{316}{}{Anything else going around?}

# Directions.
{320}{}{Drink, eat, get patched up, or get parted from your money over a counter. What are you after?}
{321}{}{A drink.}
{322}{}{Supplies.}
{323}{}{A doctor.}
{324}{}{A bed.}
{325}{}{Something to eat.}
{331}{}{Moriarty's Saloon. Back of town, past the bomb; biggest shack we've got. The beer's warm and the owner's cold. Gob behind the bar is all right, for a ghoul. For anybody.}
{332}{}{Craterside Supply. Turn right inside the gate and keep going, past the sheriff's and the water plant. Moira's fair. If she says "experiment," leave.}
{333}{}{The clinic's on your right as you come up from the gate. Doc Church. He'll fix you, and make you feel bad for needing it.}
{334}{}{The Common House, left of the pool, with MOTEL on it. The door faces the Brass Lantern. Beds are free and so are the fleas. Or Nova at the saloon rents a room, if you've money to burn.}
{335}{}{Brass Lantern, left of the path as you come in. Jenny Stahl does the serving. The stew's hot. That's all anybody promises.}

# Rumours: 340 + n, n as in rumour_fits (mgsettlr.ssl). 351 is always news.
{340}{}{That suit left town in a temper. Men like that don't come back; they send somebody. If I were you I'd watch the road.}
{341}{}{Somebody was down at the bomb in the night. I heard the access plate go. I haven't told the sheriff. Somebody should.}
{342}{}{The boy, Harden, says he'll wear the star now. He's ten. I don't know whether to laugh or go and help him.}
{343}{}{The news is you. Moriarty's already telling people the whole thing was his idea.}
{344}{}{Sheriff wants that bomb made safe, and he'd pay. Trouble is, the only folk here who understand it are the ones who pray to it.}
{345}{}{There's a man in a suit who sits in the corner of the saloon all day. Barely drinks. Watches the door. Nobody knows what he's waiting for.}
{346}{}{Walter's pipes are leaking in three places. He'd pay anybody who can swing a wrench. He's at the water plant, swearing.}
{347}{}{If you need to know a thing, Moriarty knows it. He'll even tell you. It's the price that hurts.}
{348}{}{Lucy West sits by the saloon door most days with a letter she can't afford to send. Sad thing to watch.}
{349}{}{Moira at Craterside poked about inside the bomb once and wrote it all down. She's still got every finger, so she must know something.}
{350}{}{They say the sheriff keeps enough guns in his closet to hold the wall for a week. Nobody's seen inside. Nobody's wanted to ask.}
{351}{}{Micky, outside the gate, used to guard caravans. Then he drank from the puddle. He'd give his teeth for a flask of clean water, if he could spare any.}

# Why live beside a bomb.
{360}{}{Raiders won't come within a mile of it. Slavers neither. You want safe, you live next to the thing everybody else is scared of. Also, the rent.}
{361}{}{The bomb used to keep the raiders off. Now we'll have to do it ourselves. I preferred the bomb. It didn't need feeding.}
{362}{}{Raiders won't come near it. That used to be all the reason I needed. This week I'm not so sure. Ask me again when my teeth stop itching.}

# Low Intelligence.
{370}{}{Hullo yourself. You stand over there, friend. Away from the bomb. Further. That's it. Good.}
{371}{}{Me stand.}
